{
  "image_name": "4a930e3dcada85a7c474556e25804da7.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes endocannabinoid signaling between two adjacent cells. It shows how the two main endocannabinoids, anandamide and 2-AG, are synthesized in one cell, released into the space between cells, and then act on receptors on the neighboring cell before being taken up and degraded.\n\nAnandamide is produced from NAPE through NAPE-PLD, while 2-AG is generated from DAG through DAGL. After release, both molecules bind membrane targets including CB1, CB2, and GPR55, and anandamide also appears to activate TRPV1. The arrows indicate overlapping but not identical receptor interactions for the two lipids. EMT is shown as a transport step involved in uptake across the membrane.\n\nInside the receiving cell, the compounds are broken down by distinct enzymes. Anandamide is hydrolyzed by FAAH into arachidonate and ethanolamine, whereas 2-AG is hydrolyzed by MAGL into arachidonate and glycerol. Overall, the figure highlights a full cycle of endocannabinoid production, receptor signaling, cellular uptake, and enzymatic inactivation, emphasizing that anandamide and 2-AG share some targets but are synthesized and degraded through different pathways.",
